                <pb facs="0088" n="48" xml:id="pg48"/>

                <head>26. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On December 5, 1963, President Lyndon B. Johnson chaired the first National
                    Security Council meeting of his administration. Because there was no Vice
                    President, the President invited Representative Sam Rayburn of Texas to attend
                    National Security Council meetings “from time to time as his schedule
                    permitted.” Rayburn was Johnson’s successor in the event of the President’s
                    death in office. For an outline of remarks Bundy prepared for the President and
                    the summary record of this 520th NSC meeting, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v08/d149"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1961–1963, volume VIII, Documents 149</ref> and <ref
                        target="frus:frus1961-63v08/d150">150</ref>. See also Smith, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Organizational History of the National Security
                        Council</hi>, page 57.</p>
            </div>
